		'option_b' => 'Retract the report solely to preserve the therapeutic alliance.',
		'option_c' => 'Promise never to make another report if the client returns.',
		'option_d' => 'Refuse to discuss the rupture because mandated reporting is only a legal issue.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Legal compliance and therapeutic care continue together; the clinician should not undo or disclaim a lawful report but should address its clinical impact and continuity of care.\n\nB. Incorrect. A report cannot be withdrawn simply to repair trust.\n\nC. Incorrect. A future promise to ignore mandatory duties is improper.\n\nD. Incorrect. The relational impact is clinically relevant and should be addressed.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client offers the LPCC a modest handmade item at the end of treatment. What is the BEST ethical analysis?',
		'option_a' => 'Accept every gift because declining is always culturally insensitive.',
		'option_b' => 'Reject every gift because all gifts create an unethical dual relationship.',
		'option_c' => 'Consider the gift\'s cultural and relational meaning, value, motivation, timing, power dynamics, potential harm, and clinic policy before accepting or declining.',
		'option_d' => 'Accept only if the client agrees to extend treatment for another month.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Automatic acceptance ignores risk and professional context.\n\nB. Incorrect. Automatic rejection ignores cultural and relational meaning.\n\nC. Correct. Gift decisions require contextual analysis rather than an absolute rule.\n\nD. Incorrect. Linking acceptance to more treatment is exploitative.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which threshold generally triggers a California mandated reporter\'s duty to report suspected child abuse?',
		'option_a' => 'Reasonable suspicion based on facts that would cause a reasonable person in a like position to suspect child abuse or neglect.',
		'option_b' => 'Proof beyond a reasonable doubt that abuse occurred.',
		'option_c' => 'A direct confession by the suspected perpetrator.',
		'option_d' => 'Physical evidence verified by law enforcement.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. CANRA is triggered by reasonable suspicion; mandated reporters report suspected abuse rather than proving it.\n\nB. Incorrect. Criminal-proof standards are far higher than the reporting threshold.\n\nC. Incorrect. A perpetrator confession is not required.\n\nD. Incorrect. Law-enforcement verification is not a prerequisite to reporting.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'What is the MOST ethical approach to evaluating a supervisee or trainee?',
		'option_a' => 'Save all negative feedback for the final day so the supervisee is not discouraged.',
		'option_b' => 'Base the evaluation mainly on whether the supervisor personally likes the supervisee.',
		'option_c' => 'Provide ongoing, accurate, timely feedback tied to known criteria, document significant concerns, and address remediation before a final high-stakes evaluation whenever feasible.',
		'option_d' => 'Inflate ratings to avoid difficult conversations.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Delayed surprise feedback undermines opportunity for growth and remediation.\n\nB. Incorrect. Personal preference is not a fair competency criterion.\n\nC. Correct. Evaluation should be fair, transparent, developmental, and connected to actual performance and known standards.\n\nD. Incorrect. Inflated ratings can endanger clients and distort gatekeeping.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C5 Clinical Supervision, Feedback, and Accountability",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An adult client has substantially diminished decision-making capacity but can still express preferences and understand parts of the counseling process. What is the MOST ethical approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Exclude the client from all decisions once capacity is impaired.',
		'option_b' => 'Seek legally appropriate consent while involving the client in decisions to the greatest extent possible and obtaining assent when feasible.',
		'option_c' => 'Treat any expression of preference as full legal consent regardless of capacity.',
		'option_d' => 'Allow the caregiver to make every decision without considering the client\'s wishes.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Diminished capacity does not erase personhood or the value of participation.\n\nB. Correct. Counselors protect legal consent requirements while preserving the client's voice and participation as much as possible.\n\nC. Incorrect. Preference and legal capacity are related but not identical concepts.\n\nD. Incorrect. Surrogate authority should not unnecessarily displace the client's remaining autonomy.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C1 Foundations of Informed Consent",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC treated a client who was 15 when treatment ended. What minimum retention framework applies under BPC 4999.75?',
		'option_a' => 'Retain only until the client turns 18.',
		'option_b' => 'Retain for seven years from intake regardless of the client\'s age.',
		'option_c' => 'Destroy at age 21 because minor records have a shorter period.',
		'option_d' => 'Retain the records for at least seven years from the date the client reaches age 18.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The statute extends beyond the eighteenth birthday.\n\nB. Incorrect. The period is not measured from intake.\n\nC. Incorrect. The statute does not establish the proposed age-21 destruction rule.\n\nD. Correct. For a minor, the statutory period runs at least seven years from the date the client reaches age 18.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB9-C3 Record Retention Requirements",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Six years after counseling ends, an LPCC considers a romantic relationship with a former client. Which statement is MOST accurate ethically?',
		'option_a' => 'The relationship is automatically ethical once five years pass.',
		'option_b' => 'The five-year prohibition has elapsed, but the counselor still bears a high burden to examine exploitation, harm, power imbalance, treatment history, and whether the relationship is ethically defensible.',
		'option_c' => 'The relationship is automatically prohibited forever under the ACA Code.',
		'option_d' => 'Only the former client\'s consent matters after five years.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Five years is not an automatic ethical safe harbor.\n\nB. Correct. Elapsed time ends the categorical five-year prohibition but does not erase the duty to avoid exploitation and evaluate the exceptional circumstances carefully.\n\nC. Incorrect. The current ACA Code does not impose an absolute lifetime prohibition.\n\nD. Incorrect. Consent alone does not neutralize power and exploitation concerns.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C3 Multiple and Dual Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Three months into counseling, the LPCC proposes a substantially different treatment approach with new foreseeable risks. What should happen NEXT?',
		'option_a' => 'Proceed because consent obtained at intake covers all later treatment changes.',
		'option_b' => 'Ask the client to sign a blank consent addendum and complete the details afterward.',
		'option_c' => 'Explain the material change, risks, benefits, alternatives, and client choices, obtain ongoing informed consent, and document the discussion.',
		'option_d' => 'End treatment because changing an approach automatically requires referral.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Initial consent is not unlimited permission for materially different care.\n\nB. Incorrect. Blank or incomplete consent documents do not support informed choice.\n\nC. Correct. Material changes can require renewed informed-consent discussion so the client can continue to participate voluntarily and knowingly.\n\nD. Incorrect. A treatment change does not automatically require termination when the clinician is competent and the client consents.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C3 Ongoing Informed Consent",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC begins offering encrypted messaging between sessions but does not understand the platform\'s privacy settings or retention behavior. What should the LPCC do?',
		'option_a' => 'Gain sufficient technology competence, understand privacy and record implications, set clear communication policies, and use the tool only when it can be managed safely.',
		'option_b' => 'Use the platform because the vendor describes it as secure.',
		'option_c' => 'Let clients determine all settings because they accepted the service.',
		'option_d' => 'Avoid documenting messages so they do not become part of the record.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Counselors using technology remain responsible for understanding relevant risks, limitations, and professional obligations.\n\nB. Incorrect. Vendor marketing does not replace professional evaluation.\n\nC. Incorrect. Clients cannot assume the clinician's technology duties.\n\nD. Incorrect. Record duties are not avoided by intentionally failing to document relevant communications.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C4 Technology, Electronic Communication, and Professional Responsibilities",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A probation-referred client asks exactly what the LPCC will report to probation. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Clarify before counseling what information is required or may be shared, with whom, the limits of confidentiality, and foreseeable consequences of refusing the mandated service.',
		'option_b' => 'Promise that nothing will be disclosed without a new court order.',
		'option_c' => 'State that probation automatically receives the complete clinical record.',
		'option_d' => 'Wait until probation requests information before discussing disclosure limits.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Mandated clients need advance clarity about the reporting relationship and limits of confidentiality.\n\nB. Incorrect. An absolute promise may conflict with mandated or authorized disclosures.\n\nC. Incorrect. Court involvement does not automatically authorize unlimited record disclosure.\n\nD. Incorrect. Disclosure expectations should be explained before treatment proceeds.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C4 Required Disclosures",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client wants to prioritize improved sleep while the LPCC initially believes relationship conflict is the more important target. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Replace the client\'s goal with the counselor\'s preferred goal without discussion.',
		'option_b' => 'Agree to any client goal even if it is unrelated to counseling or creates serious risk.',
		'option_c' => 'Discuss the clinical formulation transparently and collaborate on goals that respect the client\'s priorities while addressing relevant risks and treatment needs.',
		'option_d' => 'Terminate because differing priorities mean the therapeutic relationship cannot work.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Unilateral control undermines self-determination.\n\nB. Incorrect. Client autonomy exists within lawful and clinically appropriate boundaries.\n\nC. Correct. Goal setting should combine professional judgment with client autonomy and collaboration.\n\nD. Incorrect. Differences in priority are ordinarily addressed through collaborative discussion.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C5 Professional Judgment and Ethical Decision-Making",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC\'s website states "Guaranteed cure for panic disorder in three sessions." What is the PRIMARY ethical problem?',
		'option_a' => 'The website uses too few technical terms.',
		'option_b' => 'The statement is acceptable if it increases access to care.',
		'option_c' => 'The statement is misleading and makes an unsupported guarantee about professional services.',
		'option_d' => 'Professional advertising is outside ethical standards.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Technical language is not the central issue.\n\nB. Incorrect. Access goals do not justify unsupported guarantees.\n\nC. Correct. Public statements about professional services must be accurate and not misleading or deceptive.\n\nD. Incorrect. Ethical and legal duties apply to advertising and public communication.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C5 Social Media, Boundaries, and Digital Ethics",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client communicates to the LPCC a serious threat of physical violence against a reasonably identifiable person. What legal issue should the LPCC evaluate immediately?',
		'option_a' => 'Whether the LPCC may ignore the threat because confidentiality always controls.',
		'option_b' => 'The statutory duty to protect under Civil Code 43.92 and the reasonable protective steps required by the facts.',
		'option_c' => 'Whether the client has a criminal record, because only prior offenders create a duty.',
		'option_d' => 'Whether the intended victim is already aware of the threat, because awareness automatically eliminates all duties.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Confidentiality is subject to specific protective exceptions.\n\nB. Correct. A communicated serious threat against a reasonably identifiable victim triggers analysis of California's duty-to-protect statute.\n\nC. Incorrect. A prior criminal history is not the statutory trigger.\n\nD. Incorrect. Victim awareness does not automatically resolve all required protective action.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An APCC creates a public professional profile. Which information set is MOST consistent with current California advertising rules?',
		'option_a' => 'First name only and the phrase "therapist," with no registration number.',
		'option_b' => 'The supervisor\'s license number instead of the APCC\'s own registration number.',
		'option_c' => 'The title "LPCC" because the APCC is working toward licensure.',
		'option_d' => 'Accurate name, registered title or accepted abbreviation, registration number, employer or entity information as required, and clear supervised-status language.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. An APCC must accurately identify registered status and number.\n\nB. Incorrect. The registrant's own status and number cannot be replaced by the supervisor's identifier.\n\nC. Incorrect. An APCC may not represent themself as already licensed.\n\nD. Correct. Current advertising rules require accurate professional status and identifying information so the public is not misled about registration and supervision.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U4; WB2-C5 Social Media, Boundaries, and Digital Ethics",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client asks to exchange professional landscaping services for counseling. What is the MOST ethical response?',
		'option_a' => 'Agree whenever the services equal the counselor\'s hourly fee.',
		'option_b' => 'Refuse because ethical codes prohibit all bartering.',
		'option_c' => 'Consider whether bartering is culturally appropriate, not exploitative, clinically appropriate, clearly valued, and unlikely to harm the counseling relationship before agreeing.',
		'option_d' => 'Use bartering only when the counselor personally needs the service urgently.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Equal nominal value alone does not resolve the relational risks.\n\nB. Incorrect. The current ACA Code permits bartering under limited conditions.\n\nC. Correct. Bartering is not categorically prohibited but requires careful analysis of exploitation, value, culture, and clinical impact.\n\nD. Incorrect. Counselor need should not drive a potentially exploitative arrangement.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'At intake, an adult client says, "I signed the forms, so we can skip the consent discussion." What should the LPCC do?',
		'option_a' => 'Treat the signed form as complete consent and begin counseling immediately.',
		'option_b' => 'Discuss only confidentiality because other information can be provided later.',
		'option_c' => 'Delay consent information until the client asks a specific question.',
		'option_d' => 'Review the counseling relationship and process, material risks and benefits, fees, confidentiality limits, and other information needed for an informed choice, then document the discussion.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A signature can document consent but does not replace disclosure and understanding.\n\nB. Incorrect. Confidentiality is only one part of informed consent.\n\nC. Incorrect. Material information should be provided before or as services begin, not only after a problem arises.\n\nD. Correct. Informed consent is an ongoing communication process; a signature alone does not establish meaningful understanding.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C2 Information Required for Informed Decision-Making",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'When Civil Code 43.92 applies, which action pattern is specifically identified as a way to discharge the duty and obtain the statute\'s protection?',
		'option_a' => 'Document the threat privately but make no outside communication.',
		'option_b' => 'Make reasonable efforts to communicate the threat to the identifiable victim or victims and to a law-enforcement agency.',
		'option_c' => 'Notify only the client\'s family regardless of who is threatened.',
		'option_d' => 'Terminate treatment immediately without taking any protective action.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Private documentation alone does not perform the protective communications described by statute.\n\nB. Correct. The statute expressly identifies reasonable efforts to warn the victim or victims and notify law enforcement.\n\nC. Incorrect. Family notification is not a substitute for the identified statutory communications.\n\nD. Incorrect. Termination alone does not discharge the protective duty.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC asks a specialist for one-time consultation about a client. What should remain clear?',
		'option_a' => 'The consultant becomes the treating clinician automatically.',
		'option_b' => 'The original LPCC no longer needs to evaluate the consultant\'s recommendations.',
		'option_c' => 'Consultation eliminates the need to protect client confidentiality.',
		'option_d' => 'The consultation does not automatically transfer treatment responsibility; roles, confidentiality, recommendations, and follow-up responsibilities should be understood and documented as appropriate.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A consultation alone does not create a new treating relationship.\n\nB. Incorrect. The treating clinician retains responsibility for professional judgment.\n\nC. Incorrect. Consultation disclosures remain subject to confidentiality safeguards.\n\nD. Correct. Consultation supports decision-making but does not automatically change treatment responsibility or confidentiality duties.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C4 Consultation and Seeking Professional Assistance",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is invited to speak at a conference. Which bio is MOST ethical?',
		'option_a' => 'A bio that lists a pending certification as already earned.',
		'option_b' => 'A bio that uses a protected title because the audience probably will not verify it.',
		'option_c' => 'One that accurately states current licenses, credentials, training, and areas of competence without implying qualifications the counselor does not hold.',
		'option_d' => 'A bio that omits limitations and exaggerates specialty experience to attract referrals.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Pending qualifications should not be represented as completed.\n\nB. Incorrect. Protected titles and credentials must be accurate.\n\nC. Correct. Professional representation should be accurate and nonmisleading.\n\nD. Incorrect. Exaggeration can mislead the public and distort referral decisions.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C6 Professional Roles and Role Clarification",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'At the beginning of a routine telehealth session, what client information must the California LPCC obtain and document under current regulation?',
		'option_a' => 'The client\'s full name and the address of the client\'s present location.',
		'option_b' => 'Only the client\'s city because a street address is never required.',
		'option_c' => 'Only the device IP address if the platform captures it automatically.',
		'option_d' => 'The client\'s employer address, regardless of where the client is physically located.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Current California telehealth regulation requires verbal acquisition and documentation of the client's full name and present-location address at the beginning of each telehealth service.\n\nB. Incorrect. The regulation requires the address of the present location, not merely the city.\n\nC. Incorrect. An IP address is not a substitute for the required verbal identity/location information.\n\nD. Incorrect. The relevant address is where the client is physically located for that service.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C1 Telehealth Foundations",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client has limited literacy. What is the MOST ethical way to obtain informed consent?',
		'option_a' => 'Provide the standard written form only and assume a signature proves understanding.',
		'option_b' => 'Ask a family member to sign for the client even though the client has decision-making capacity.',
		'option_c' => 'Skip consent because simplifying the information could be patronizing.',
		'option_d' => 'Present the information in language and formats the client can understand, check comprehension, invite questions, and document the process.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A signature does not establish comprehension.\n\nB. Incorrect. Capacity should not be displaced merely because literacy is limited.\n\nC. Incorrect. Accessible communication strengthens rather than diminishes informed choice.\n\nD. Correct. Informed consent requires meaningful understanding, so communication should be adapted to the client's needs.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C1 Foundations of Informed Consent",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client is making a life decision that conflicts with the LPCC\'s personal values but is not illegal or inherently dangerous. What is the MOST ethical response?',
		'option_a' => 'Persuade the client to choose the counselor\'s preferred option.',
		'option_b' => 'Terminate immediately because value differences make counseling impossible.',
		'option_c' => 'Pretend to agree with the client while covertly steering treatment toward the counselor\'s values.',
		'option_d' => 'Monitor personal values, support the client\'s autonomous decision-making, and seek consultation if the counselor\'s values are interfering with care.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Persuasion based on counselor values is not client-centered.\n\nB. Incorrect. Value difference alone does not require termination.\n\nC. Incorrect. Covert steering is still values imposition.\n\nD. Correct. Counselors avoid imposing personal values and protect client autonomy while managing their own reactions.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB7-C2 Bias, Assumptions, and Ethical Decision-Making",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC repeatedly interprets a client\'s culturally normative spiritual experiences as psychotic symptoms despite collateral information and cultural consultation suggesting otherwise. What is the PRIMARY ethical concern?',
		'option_a' => 'The counselor must keep the original diagnosis because changing it would show uncertainty.',
		'option_b' => 'Bias may be distorting assessment and diagnosis, requiring culturally informed reassessment, consultation, and correction to protect the client from harm.',
		'option_c' => 'Cultural information is irrelevant once a DSM label has been considered.',
		'option_d' => 'The client must prove the spiritual practice is culturally accepted before the counselor can reconsider.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Accuracy is more important than preserving apparent certainty.\n\nB. Correct. Diagnostic competence includes cultural context and willingness to revise a formulation when bias or unsupported assumptions are identified.\n\nC. Incorrect. Cultural context can materially affect interpretation of symptoms.\n\nD. Incorrect. The clinician bears responsibility for competent assessment rather than shifting the burden to the client.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB7-C2 Bias, Assumptions, and Ethical Decision-Making",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A 78-year-old client reports that a caregiver is taking signed blank checks and making unauthorized withdrawals. The LPCC reasonably suspects financial abuse. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Keep the information confidential because financial abuse is not reportable unless physical injury occurs.',
		'option_b' => 'Make the required elder/dependent-adult abuse report promptly to the appropriate agency and complete any required follow-up reporting.',
		'option_c' => 'Question the caregiver first to determine whether the withdrawals were justified.',
		'option_d' => 'Wait for bank records proving the theft.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Financial abuse is a recognized reportable category.\n\nB. Correct. California elder/dependent-adult reporting law includes financial abuse and uses a suspicion-based reporting framework.\n\nC. Incorrect. The mandated reporter is not expected to investigate the suspected perpetrator before reporting.\n\nD. Incorrect. Documentary proof is not required before the duty arises.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An intervention is generally within the LPCC profession\'s statutory scope, but an individual LPCC has never been trained to provide it. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Use the intervention because anything within LPCC scope is automatically within every LPCC\'s competence.',
		'option_b' => 'Avoid the intervention forever because lack of current competence can never be remedied.',
		'option_c' => 'Ask the client to sign a waiver accepting the clinician\'s lack of competence.',
		'option_d' => 'Distinguish profession-wide scope from individual competence and obtain appropriate training, consultation, supervision, or referral before using the intervention.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Profession-wide authorization does not establish individual competence.\n\nB. Incorrect. Competence can be developed through appropriate education, training, and supervised experience.\n\nC. Incorrect. A waiver cannot substitute for professional competence.\n\nD. Correct. Legal scope answers what the profession may do; competence answers what this clinician is prepared to do safely and effectively.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C2 Scope of Practice vs. Scope of Competence",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is considering attending a long-term client\'s culturally significant public ceremony. What is the BEST ethical analysis?',
		'option_a' => 'Consider the potential benefit and harm, power differential, cultural meaning, role expectations, alternatives, and documentation before deciding.',
		'option_b' => 'Decline automatically because every out-of-office contact is unethical.',
		'option_c' => 'Attend automatically because the client requested it.',
		'option_d' => 'Attend without discussion so the event remains spontaneous.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Boundary extensions are not judged by a simple location rule; they require contextual analysis of benefit, risk, power, and role clarity.\n\nB. Incorrect. Not every boundary extension is prohibited.\n\nC. Incorrect. Client request alone does not resolve professional risk.\n\nD. Incorrect. Advance discussion can clarify expectations and reduce harm.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C1 Professional Boundaries",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'What is an essential ethical feature of a planned private-practice closure?',
		'option_a' => 'Immediate destruction of every record on the last business day.',
		'option_b' => 'A process for secure record custody, lawful retention, client notice and access, continuity or referral, and eventual appropriate disposition of records.',
		'option_c' => 'Leaving records with the landlord because the office lease has ended.',
		'option_d' => 'Keeping the closure secret from clients to avoid anxiety.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Retention duties continue after the practice closes.\n\nB. Correct. Practice closure requires protection of records and reasonable continuity arrangements rather than abandonment of professional responsibilities.\n\nC. Incorrect. A landlord is not automatically an authorized record custodian.\n\nD. Incorrect. Clients need appropriate notice and access information.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB9-C4 Record Security, Storage, and Record Destruction",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'When may the dangerous-patient exception to psychotherapist-patient privilege apply?',
		'option_a' => 'Whenever a patient is angry, even without any danger concern.',
		'option_b' => 'Only when a patient has already committed a violent crime.',
		'option_c' => 'When the therapist has reasonable cause to believe the patient is dangerous to self or to the person or property of another and disclosure is necessary to prevent the threatened danger.',
		'option_d' => 'Only when danger is directed at the therapist personally.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Anger alone is not the statutory standard.\n\nB. Incorrect. The exception is preventive and does not require a completed crime.\n\nC. Correct. Evidence Code 1024 ties the exception to reasonable cause of danger and necessity of disclosure to prevent the threatened danger.\n\nD. Incorrect. The danger can concern the patient or another person or property, not only the therapist.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C1 Confidentiality in Individual, Couple, Family, and Group Treatment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC notices that personal cultural assumptions may be shaping interpretation of a client\'s family behavior. What should the LPCC do NEXT?',
		'option_a' => 'Defend the original interpretation because clinicians must appear confident.',
		'option_b' => 'Examine the assumptions, seek culturally informed consultation or learning, invite the client\'s perspective, and revise the formulation as needed.',
		'option_c' => 'Ask the client to adopt the counselor\'s cultural norms for treatment to work.',
		'option_d' => 'Avoid discussing culture because it could introduce bias into the session.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Confidence should not protect an unsupported formulation.\n\nB. Correct. Cultural humility requires active self-examination and client-centered correction of assumptions.\n\nC. Incorrect. Imposing counselor norms can distort care.\n\nD. Incorrect. Ignoring culture can itself perpetuate bias.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB7-C2 Bias, Assumptions, and Ethical Decision-Making",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client describes a credible plan to burn down an occupied business, and the LPCC has reasonable cause to believe disclosure is necessary to prevent the threatened danger. Which privilege rule is MOST relevant?',
		'option_a' => 'Privilege can be overcome only by danger to a named person, never property.',
		'option_b' => 'The exception applies only after the threatened property has actually been damaged.',
		'option_c' => 'Because the threat concerns property, the clinician must maintain privilege regardless of danger to people.',
		'option_d' => 'The Evidence Code 1024 dangerous-patient exception can include danger to the person or property of another when disclosure is necessary to prevent the threatened danger.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The statutory language is not limited to danger against a named person.\n\nB. Incorrect. The exception is preventive and does not require completed harm.\n\nC. Incorrect. A property-directed plan may also threaten persons and is not categorically excluded from the statute.\n\nD. Correct. Evidence Code 1024 expressly includes danger to the person or property of another and focuses on preventive necessity.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C1 Confidentiality in Individual, Couple, Family, and Group Treatment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An adult client properly requests an electronic copy of records the LPCC maintains electronically in a readily producible format. Which action BEST follows California law?',
		'option_a' => 'Use a universal 30-day response period for all California record requests.',
		'option_b' => 'Require in-person inspection before providing any copy.',
		'option_c' => 'Provide the readily producible electronic copy and transmit it within the applicable 15-day period.',
		'option_d' => 'Convert the file to paper because electronic delivery is never required.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The cited California provision does not impose a universal 30-day rule.\n\nB. Incorrect. Inspection is a separate right and is not a prerequisite to receiving a copy.\n\nC. Correct. The statute addresses both the available format and a 15-day transmission period for copies.\n\nD. Incorrect. Electronic copies are contemplated when the records are maintained electronically.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C4 Client Access to Records and Responding to Record Requests",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which statement BEST describes the statutory scope of professional clinical counseling in California?',
		'option_a' => 'It includes applying counseling interventions and psychotherapeutic techniques to identify and remediate cognitive, mental, emotional, behavioral, and relational issues within the profession\'s lawful scope.',
		'option_b' => 'It is limited to career counseling and excludes psychotherapy.',
		'option_c' => 'It permits any medical procedure if it supports a counseling goal.',
		'option_d' => 'It is identical to the scope of every other behavioral-health license.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. BPC 4999.20 defines professional clinical counseling broadly enough to include psychotherapy and counseling interventions, while preserving profession-specific boundaries.\n\nB. Incorrect. California LPCC practice is not limited to career counseling.\n\nC. Incorrect. Medical procedures are not added to LPCC scope merely because they support counseling.\n\nD. Incorrect. Behavioral-health licenses have distinct statutory scopes and requirements.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U2; WB3-C2 Scope of Practice vs. Scope of Competence",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An older adult with capacity asks the LPCC not to report suspected caregiver abuse because "I will lose my housing." The statutory reporting threshold is met. What should the LPCC do?',
		'option_a' => 'Honor the request because competent adults can always veto mandated reports.',
		'option_b' => 'Explain the reporting duty and make the mandated report while using clinically appropriate steps to address safety, housing concerns, and therapeutic impact.',
		'option_c' => 'Delay the report until a replacement residence is found.',
		'option_d' => 'Document the request and make no external report if the client signs a waiver.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Capacity does not create a veto over a mandated report.\n\nB. Correct. A client's preference is clinically important but does not cancel a mandatory reporting duty once the statutory threshold is met.\n\nC. Incorrect. The reporting timeline cannot be postponed for housing planning.\n\nD. Incorrect. A waiver cannot eliminate a statutory duty.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC discovers a factual error in yesterday\'s progress note. What is the BEST way to correct it?',
		'option_a' => 'Use the record system\'s correction or addendum process so the original entry remains traceable and the correction is dated, accurate, and attributable.',
		'option_b' => 'Delete the original note without leaving any audit trail.',
		'option_c' => 'Change the note silently so it appears the error never existed.',
		'option_d' => 'Create a second contradictory note without identifying it as a correction.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Clinical records should preserve integrity and an understandable history of corrections.\n\nB. Incorrect. Erasing the original can undermine record integrity.\n\nC. Incorrect. Silent alteration obscures the chronology and author accountability.\n\nD. Incorrect. An unidentified contradictory entry creates confusion rather than a transparent correction.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB9-C2 Documentation Errors, Risk Management, and High-Risk Situations",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC collaborates frequently with a psychiatrist and receives no payment for referrals. Which arrangement is MOST ethically sound?',
		'option_a' => 'Agree to send a minimum number of clients each month in exchange for gift cards.',
		'option_b' => 'Refer based on client need and quality of care, disclose relevant relationships when appropriate, protect confidentiality, and avoid compensation merely for referrals.',
		'option_c' => 'Choose the psychiatrist solely because the psychiatrist sends referrals back.',
		'option_d' => 'Share complete client records automatically with every referral.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Gift-card compensation creates a referral incentive.\n\nB. Correct. Interprofessional collaboration can be appropriate when driven by client welfare rather than reciprocal financial inducement or unnecessary disclosure.\n\nC. Incorrect. Reciprocity alone is not a client-centered referral criterion.\n\nD. Incorrect. Referral does not automatically authorize disclosure of the complete record.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client revokes a valid release authorizing monthly updates to an attorney before the next update is sent. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Send one more update because the authorization was valid when originally signed.',
		'option_b' => 'Continue sending updates until the attorney acknowledges the revocation.',
		'option_c' => 'Erase documentation of all prior disclosures made under the authorization.',
		'option_d' => 'Stop future disclosures under that authorization, document the revocation, and evaluate whether any independent legal authority requires or permits disclosure.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Prior validity does not authorize future disclosure after revocation.\n\nB. Incorrect. The recipient does not have to approve the client's revocation.\n\nC. Incorrect. Prior lawful disclosure records should not be destroyed.\n\nD. Correct. Revocation ends reliance on that authorization for future disclosures while leaving prior lawful disclosures intact.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C2 Authorizations, Releases of Information, and Limits on Disclosure",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A former client requests a copy of the treatment record but still owes the LPCC $450. Which action is MOST appropriate?',
		'option_a' => 'Process the lawful record request and address the unpaid balance separately.',
		'option_b' => 'Withhold all records until the account is paid.',
		'option_c' => 'Release only the diagnosis page until the balance is resolved.',
		'option_d' => 'Require a payment plan before starting the record-copy process.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. California patient-record law does not permit withholding records because of an unpaid bill for health care services.\n\nB. Incorrect. Collection rights do not override the statutory access duty.\n\nC. Incorrect. Partial release based only on debt does not satisfy the access obligation.\n\nD. Incorrect. A payment arrangement cannot be imposed as a precondition to a lawful request.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C4 Client Access to Records and Responding to Record Requests",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which policy element is MOST important when an LPCC communicates clinically by text or portal message?',
		'option_a' => 'Promise immediate 24/7 responses to all messages regardless of actual availability.',
		'option_b' => 'Clarify appropriate uses, expected response times, privacy limitations, emergency alternatives, record treatment, and how communication will be monitored.',
		'option_c' => 'Use texting only for emergencies and provide no alternative crisis instructions.',
		'option_d' => 'Avoid any written policy so communication can remain flexible.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Promises should reflect realistic service capacity.\n\nB. Correct. Electronic communication should be structured by clear expectations that address privacy, emergencies, response times, and documentation.\n\nC. Incorrect. Texting is not necessarily an appropriate emergency system and clients need alternatives.\n\nD. Incorrect. Lack of policy increases misunderstanding and risk.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C4 Technology, Electronic Communication, and Professional Responsibilities",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An APCC with a legally required supervisor receives helpful weekly advice from an experienced colleague who is not the designated supervisor. Which statement is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'Consultation can supplement learning, but it does not replace the legally qualifying supervision relationship, required oversight, documentation, and supervisor accountability.',
		'option_b' => 'Any experienced colleague can function as the legal supervisor if the advice is clinically sound.',
		'option_c' => 'Consultation automatically satisfies supervision-hour requirements.',
		'option_d' => 'The APCC should stop consultation because associates may speak only with their supervisor.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Consultation and legal supervision serve different functions; helpful consultation does not substitute for required supervision controls.\n\nB. Incorrect. Clinical expertise alone does not create a qualifying legal supervision relationship.\n\nC. Incorrect. Supervision-credit rules are not satisfied merely by consultation.\n\nD. Incorrect. Appropriate consultation can be useful in addition to supervision.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U5; WB3-C5 Clinical Supervision, Feedback, and Accountability",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC will be unavailable for two weeks and has several clients with significant ongoing risk. What is the BEST preparation?',
		'option_a' => 'Give every client the same generic voicemail message and make no further plan.',
		'option_b' => 'Tell clients not to have crises during the absence.',
		'option_c' => 'Provide the LPCC\'s personal vacation location in case clients need to visit.',
		'option_d' => 'Create clinically appropriate coverage and emergency plans, communicate how clients can obtain help, and coordinate continuity based on individual risk.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A generic message may be insufficient for high-risk clients.\n\nB. Incorrect. Clients cannot control emergencies to fit a clinician's schedule.\n\nC. Incorrect. Boundary violations are not an appropriate continuity plan.\n\nD. Correct. Planned absence requires reasonable continuity measures proportionate to client need and risk.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C3 Beneficence and Promoting Client Welfare",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC needs to raise fees for ongoing private-pay clients. What is the MOST ethical approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Provide reasonable advance notice, explain the new fee and effective date, discuss options or referrals when needed, and document the change.',
		'option_b' => 'Raise the fee retroactively for sessions already provided.',
		'option_c' => 'Wait until the client receives a larger invoice to disclose the change.',
		'option_d' => 'Threaten immediate termination if the client questions the increase.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Financial terms are part of informed professional practice and should be communicated prospectively and respectfully.\n\nB. Incorrect. Retroactive changes undermine informed agreement.\n\nC. Incorrect. Surprise billing prevents informed financial choice.\n\nD. Incorrect. Coercive termination threats can exploit the power imbalance.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC ended counseling with a client three years ago and is considering a sexual relationship. Which analysis is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'Satisfying a shorter California statutory prohibition does not end the ethical analysis; the current ACA Code imposes a longer five-year prohibition and additional scrutiny, so both law and ethics must be satisfied.',
		'option_b' => 'California law always overrides ethics in a way that permits the relationship after two years.',
		'option_c' => 'The ACA Code permits a sexual relationship immediately after counseling ends if both adults consent.',
		'option_d' => 'The relationship is automatically ethical after two years because former clients are no longer vulnerable.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Professional decisions must satisfy applicable law and the governing ethical standard; the current ACA Code is more restrictive than the statutory two-year minimum in this scenario.\n\nB. Incorrect. A legal minimum does not erase a more protective ethical standard.\n\nC. Incorrect. The ACA Code does not permit immediate sexual relationships with former clients.\n\nD. Incorrect. Time alone does not establish absence of exploitation or harm.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C5 Sexual Misconduct and Client Exploitation",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'When should a counselor consider ethically terminating a counseling relationship?',
		'option_a' => 'Only when the client has paid every outstanding balance.',
		'option_b' => 'Never, because counselors must continue indefinitely once treatment starts.',
		'option_c' => 'When it is reasonably clear the client no longer needs services, is not likely to benefit, or is being harmed by continued counseling, with appropriate transition planning.',
		'option_d' => 'Immediately after any disagreement with the client.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Financial disputes should be handled ethically but do not define clinical need.\n\nB. Incorrect. Counseling is not an indefinite obligation when it is no longer beneficial.\n\nC. Correct. Ethical termination is tied to benefit, harm, need, and continuity rather than punishment or convenience.\n\nD. Incorrect. Ordinary disagreement is not a sufficient basis for abrupt termination.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C3 Beneficence and Promoting Client Welfare",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC has substantial information suggesting a colleague is engaging in a serious ethical violation that may harm clients. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Spread the allegation informally so others can decide whether it is serious.',
		'option_b' => 'Clarify the facts, consult the ethical standard and applicable law, consider informal resolution when appropriate and safe, and report or escalate through proper channels when required, while protecting confidentiality.',
		'option_c' => 'Ignore the concern unless the colleague admits wrongdoing.',
		'option_d' => 'File every possible complaint immediately without checking the facts or applicable duties.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Informal rumor-sharing can harm clients and professionals without due process.\n\nB. Correct. Ethical-violation response requires accuracy, proportionality, confidentiality, and appropriate escalation rather than gossip, avoidance, or reflexive punishment.\n\nC. Incorrect. Admission is not required before responsible action.\n\nD. Incorrect. Immediate maximal escalation may be inappropriate if facts or duties are unclear and no urgent danger exists.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C4 Professional Responsibility and Accountability; WB4-C5 Responding Ethically to Professional Impairment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC has credible evidence that a colleague\'s impairment is affecting client care. What is the BEST initial ethical approach when immediate danger is not present?',
		'option_a' => 'Assess the seriousness, consult as appropriate, encourage corrective help or limitation of practice, document relevant actions, and escalate or report when required to protect clients.',
		'option_b' => 'Ignore the issue because colleague impairment is private.',
		'option_c' => 'Publicly accuse the colleague on social media to warn the community.',
		'option_d' => 'Assume impairment automatically proves professional misconduct without further assessment.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Ethical response balances client protection, fairness, consultation, remediation, and any applicable reporting duties.\n\nB. Incorrect. Client welfare can make colleague impairment a professional concern.\n\nC. Incorrect. Public accusation is disproportionate and risks confidentiality and fairness.\n\nD. Incorrect. Functional impact should be assessed rather than presumed.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB4-C5 Responding Ethically to Professional Impairment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'After developing reasonable suspicion of reportable child abuse, what reporting sequence generally applies?',
		'option_a' => 'Wait for corroboration and submit one written report within seven days.',
		'option_b' => 'Tell a supervisor and allow the supervisor to decide whether an outside report is needed.',
		'option_c' => 'Document the concern and report only if the child later confirms it.',
		'option_d' => 'Make the initial report immediately or as soon as practicably possible by telephone and complete the written follow-up within 36 hours.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The statute does not require corroboration before reporting.\n\nB. Incorrect. Internal consultation does not replace the reporter's statutory duty.\n\nC. Incorrect. Reasonable suspicion, not later confirmation, triggers the report.\n\nD. Correct. CANRA specifies prompt telephone reporting followed by a written report within 36 hours.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client asks the LPCC to write a letter stating the client is "certainly unable to work for the next year," but the LPCC has not assessed occupational functioning to that degree. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Write the requested conclusion because helping the client requires advocacy.',
		'option_b' => 'Use the strongest wording possible because the recipient may otherwise deny the request.',
		'option_c' => 'Explain the limits of the LPCC\'s information and role, provide only opinions supported by adequate assessment and records, or refer for an appropriate evaluation.',
		'option_d' => 'Copy the client\'s requested statement verbatim and sign it as a professional opinion.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Client benefit does not justify inaccurate representation.\n\nB. Incorrect. Exaggeration can mislead third parties and create professional risk.\n\nC. Correct. Advocacy does not permit unsupported professional conclusions; opinions should remain within competence, evidence, and role.\n\nD. Incorrect. A client-authored claim does not become a supported clinical opinion through signature.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C4 Professional Responsibility and Accountability",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is tempted to search a client\'s private social-media accounts to verify whether the client is being truthful. What is the BEST ethical analysis?',
		'option_a' => 'Search secretly because anything online is automatically fair clinical evidence.',
		'option_b' => 'Search every client routinely to make assessments more objective.',
		'option_c' => 'Create a fake account to gain access if the client\'s profile is private.',
		'option_d' => 'Consider whether the search has a legitimate clinical purpose, whether consent or policy addresses it, the client\'s privacy expectations, potential harm and boundary effects, and whether less intrusive ways can meet the need.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Availability online does not eliminate privacy and boundary concerns.\n\nB. Incorrect. Routine surveillance is disproportionate and can damage trust.\n\nC. Incorrect. Deception to gain access creates additional ethical problems.\n\nD. Correct. Online information may be accessible without being ethically neutral to obtain; purpose, privacy, transparency, and relational effects require analysis.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C5 Social Media, Boundaries, and Digital Ethics",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which action is MOST consistent with the ethical nondiscrimination standard?',
		'option_a' => 'Provide professional services without discriminating against clients based on protected or marginalized identities and address competence needs appropriately.',
		'option_b' => 'Refuse all clients whose beliefs differ from the counselor\'s values.',
		'option_c' => 'Use stereotypes as shortcuts when planning treatment.',
		'option_d' => 'Refer solely because the counselor is uncomfortable with a client\'s identity.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Ethical practice requires nondiscrimination and attention to competence rather than value-based exclusion.\n\nB. Incorrect. Value difference alone is not an ethical basis for discrimination.\n\nC. Incorrect. Stereotyping undermines accurate and culturally responsive care.\n\nD. Incorrect. Counselor discomfort should prompt self-reflection and competence work, not identity-based rejection.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB7-C1 Cultural Humility and Culturally Responsive Practice",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is asked by an employer to bill a 60-minute session when only 35 minutes of service occurred because "the payer rarely checks." What should the LPCC do?',
		'option_a' => 'Follow the employer\'s instruction because the employer owns the billing account.',
		'option_b' => 'Document and bill accurately according to the service actually provided and applicable billing rules, and address the improper directive through appropriate organizational channels.',
		'option_c' => 'Bill 60 minutes only when the client agrees in writing.',
		'option_d' => 'Alter the note to make it appear that 60 minutes occurred.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Employer direction does not eliminate individual ethical and legal duties.\n\nB. Correct. Professional accountability includes accurate records and billing; organizational pressure does not justify false representation.\n\nC. Incorrect. Client consent cannot make false billing accurate.\n\nD. Incorrect. Altering documentation compounds the misrepresentation.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Before beginning treatment, what is the BEST practice regarding fees?',
		'option_a' => 'Avoid discussing fees because money is not a clinical issue.',
		'option_b' => 'Set the fee after several sessions based on how difficult the case becomes.',
		'option_c' => 'Disclose fees and billing arrangements clearly enough for the client to make an informed financial decision before treatment begins.',
		'option_d' => 'Charge any amount later as long as the client received some services.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Financial transparency is part of informed professional practice.\n\nB. Incorrect. Retroactive or case-difficulty-based fee setting can be misleading and exploitative.\n\nC. Correct. California professional-conduct law and counseling ethics support clear advance disclosure of fees and financial arrangements.\n\nD. Incorrect. Providing services does not eliminate the duty to disclose agreed financial terms.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C4 Gifts, Bartering, and Financial Relationships",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC becomes concerned that a client may be dangerous based on behavior and collateral information, but the client has not communicated a serious threat of physical violence against an identifiable victim. Which statement is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'The specific Civil Code 43.92 communicated-threat trigger may not be met, but the LPCC must still assess risk and consider any other lawful safety interventions or disclosure exceptions that apply.',
		'option_b' => 'Civil Code 43.92 automatically applies to every clinical concern about dangerousness.',
		'option_c' => 'No safety action can ever be taken unless the client verbally names a victim.',
		'option_d' => 'The LPCC should fabricate a communicated threat in the record so protective action is permitted.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. The Tarasoff codification has a specific communicated-threat trigger, while other safety, emergency, privilege, or clinical duties may still require analysis.\n\nB. Incorrect. The statute is narrower than every possible dangerousness concern.\n\nC. Incorrect. Other legal and clinical pathways can apply even without a qualifying communicated threat.\n\nD. Incorrect. Documentation must remain accurate and cannot invent facts.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which statement BEST reflects a counselor\'s ethical duty regarding competence?',
		'option_a' => 'Accept any case within the profession\'s legal scope regardless of personal preparation.',
		'option_b' => 'Use client consent as a substitute for missing competence.',
		'option_c' => 'Practice only within the boundaries of competence based on education, training, supervised experience, credentials, and appropriate professional experience.',
		'option_d' => 'Avoid all unfamiliar client presentations because competence cannot expand.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Legal scope does not establish individual competence.\n\nB. Incorrect. Consent cannot cure a competence deficit.\n\nC. Correct. Ethical competence is individualized and can develop through appropriate education, training, consultation, and supervised experience.\n\nD. Incorrect. Counselors may expand competence through appropriate professional development.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C1 Professional Competence Foundations",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Before group counseling begins, what confidentiality step is MOST ethical?',
		'option_a' => 'Guarantee that every member can be legally forced to keep all group information secret.',
		'option_b' => 'Explain counselor confidentiality duties and the expectations and limitations regarding confidentiality among group members.',
		'option_c' => 'Avoid discussing confidentiality because doing so may make members anxious.',
		'option_d' => 'Tell members that anything said in group may be publicly shared without concern.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Counselors generally cannot guarantee every group member's confidentiality behavior.\n\nB. Correct. Group members should understand both the counselor's obligations and the practical limits on controlling other members' disclosures.\n\nC. Incorrect. Avoiding the issue weakens informed participation.\n\nD. Incorrect. Group participation does not make disclosures ethically unrestricted.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C1 Confidentiality in Individual, Couple, Family, and Group Treatment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A clinician says, "Confidentiality and privilege are the same rule, so if one exception applies the other always disappears too." Which correction is BEST?',
		'option_a' => 'The statement is correct because the terms are legally interchangeable.',
		'option_b' => 'Privilege applies only to billing information and confidentiality applies only to clinical notes.',
		'option_c' => 'Confidentiality is a broader professional/legal duty concerning disclosure, while privilege primarily governs compelled disclosure in legal proceedings; each has its own exceptions and analysis.',
		'option_d' => 'Confidentiality belongs to the court while privilege belongs to the therapist.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. They are not interchangeable concepts.\n\nB. Incorrect. Neither concept is limited in the way described.\n\nC. Correct. The two concepts overlap but serve different legal functions, so one should not be used as a substitute for the other.\n\nD. Incorrect. The patient generally holds privilege; confidentiality is not owned by the court.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C1 Confidentiality in Individual, Couple, Family, and Group Treatment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A court appoints an LPCC to examine a person specifically to report findings to the court. Which privilege principle is MOST relevant?',
		'option_a' => 'The ordinary therapy privilege always applies exactly the same way to a court-appointed evaluation.',
		'option_b' => 'Communications made in the course of the court-ordered examination may fall within the statutory court-appointed examination exception to psychotherapist-patient privilege.',
		'option_c' => 'Everything learned may be published publicly because the court ordered the examination.',
		'option_d' => 'The evaluator may expand the evaluation to unrelated treatment issues because privilege is absent.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The statute creates a defined exception in the court-appointed context.\n\nB. Correct. California Evidence Code recognizes a specific privilege exception for certain court-appointed examinations; the evaluator must still remain within the authorized purpose and other confidentiality duties.\n\nC. Incorrect. A privilege exception does not authorize public dissemination.\n\nD. Incorrect. The evaluator's role remains limited to the authorized purpose.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB8-C1 Confidentiality in Individual, Couple, Family, and Group Treatment",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An agency publishes a webpage calling an APCC "Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor" and omits the registration number and supervision status. The APCC notices the error. What should the APCC do?',
		'option_a' => 'Ignore it because only the agency is responsible for employer-created advertising.',
		'option_b' => 'Leave it unchanged if the APCC intends to become licensed soon.',
		'option_c' => 'Add the supervisor\'s name privately in the clinical chart instead of correcting the public page.',
		'option_d' => 'Take reasonable steps to have the advertisement corrected promptly because registrants remain responsible for accurate professional representation even when an employer creates the advertisement.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. The registrant should act when aware of inaccurate representation.\n\nB. Incorrect. Future licensure does not make a current false title accurate.\n\nC. Incorrect. Private chart documentation does not correct a misleading public advertisement.\n\nD. Correct. Public-facing professional status must be accurate; employer authorship does not make misrepresentation harmless to the public.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U4; WB2-C5 Social Media, Boundaries, and Digital Ethics",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which action BEST reflects the ethical duty to avoid harm?',
		'option_a' => 'Avoid every intervention that could produce temporary discomfort.',
		'option_b' => 'Guarantee that counseling will never have any unwanted effect.',
		'option_c' => 'Use reasonable professional judgment to prevent or minimize foreseeable harm while still providing appropriate care.',
		'option_d' => 'Continue an ineffective intervention because changing course might upset the client.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Therapeutic discomfort can be appropriate when proportionate and clinically justified.\n\nB. Incorrect. Counselors cannot guarantee zero risk.\n\nC. Correct. Nonmaleficence requires thoughtful risk reduction, not an impossible guarantee that treatment will never involve discomfort or uncertainty.\n\nD. Incorrect. Persisting with ineffective care can itself cause harm.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C1 The Principle of Nonmaleficence",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC uses both personal and professional social-media accounts. What is the MOST ethical approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Accept every client friend request on the personal account to strengthen rapport.',
		'option_b' => 'Maintain clear role boundaries, accurate professional information, privacy settings and policies, and avoid interactions that create confidentiality, exploitation, or misleading-identity risks.',
		'option_c' => 'Use a false professional title on the private account because only business pages count as advertising.',
		'option_d' => 'Discuss de-identified client stories freely because social media is informal.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Personal online access can create boundary and privacy complications.\n\nB. Correct. Online spaces do not remove professional responsibilities for role clarity, confidentiality, and accurate representation.\n\nC. Incorrect. Professional-status accuracy can matter across public-facing online content.\n\nD. Incorrect. Informal posting can still risk re-identification and confidentiality breaches.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C5 Social Media, Boundaries, and Digital Ethics",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An evidence-based intervention conflicts with a client\'s cultural understanding of family decision-making. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Tell the client the cultural belief must be discarded for therapy to work.',
		'option_b' => 'Explore the client\'s cultural framework, preserve the intervention\'s essential clinical purpose when possible, and collaboratively adapt delivery without stereotyping or abandoning evidence.',
		'option_c' => 'Apply the intervention rigidly because evidence-based practice cannot be adapted.',
		'option_d' => 'Assume every person from the client\'s culture holds the same beliefs.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Imposing counselor norms can damage autonomy and alliance.\n\nB. Correct. Culturally responsive practice integrates evidence, client context, and collaboration.\n\nC. Incorrect. Evidence-based care allows thoughtful adaptation when core mechanisms are preserved.\n\nD. Incorrect. Cultural identity does not justify stereotyping individuals.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB7-C3 Working With Diverse Populations",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which assessment activity is expressly outside the LPCC statutory assessment definition unless another legal basis applies?',
		'option_a' => 'Selecting a counseling-relevant screening instrument within competence.',
		'option_b' => 'Scoring a permitted assessment instrument within training.',
		'option_c' => 'Using assessment information to support counseling treatment planning.',
		'option_d' => 'Individually administered intelligence testing as an LPCC assessment function.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Permitted screening can fall within assessment when within competence and statutory bounds.\n\nB. Incorrect. Scoring permitted tools can be within scope and competence.\n\nC. Incorrect. Using lawful assessment information for treatment planning is consistent with counseling practice.\n\nD. Correct. BPC 4999.20 expressly excludes certain assessment activities, including individually administered intelligence tests, projective personality techniques, specified neuropsychological testing, and certain multi-test batteries.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U3; WB3-C2 Scope of Practice vs. Scope of Competence",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A client from a close-knit community asks the LPCC to attend a public community event where the counselor\'s presence may reveal the counseling relationship. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Explore cultural meaning, client goals, confidentiality risks, public encounter preferences, power dynamics, and alternatives before making a documented boundary decision.',
		'option_b' => 'Attend without discussion because cultural responsiveness requires accepting every invitation.',
		'option_c' => 'Decline automatically because public encounters with clients are always unethical.',
		'option_d' => 'Post about the event afterward so the client knows the counselor was proud to attend.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. The decision involves both boundary and confidentiality risks and should be made collaboratively with cultural context rather than by a universal rule.\n\nB. Incorrect. Cultural responsiveness does not eliminate risk analysis.\n\nC. Incorrect. Some boundary extensions can be ethical when carefully considered.\n\nD. Incorrect. Public posting can create or amplify confidentiality concerns.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB6-C1 Professional Boundaries; WB7-C1 Cultural Humility",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Which practice BEST protects counseling records?',
		'option_a' => 'Store all records in a shared folder accessible to every employee.',
		'option_b' => 'Use the same simple password for every clinical system to make access easier.',
		'option_c' => 'Email unencrypted records to a personal account for convenience.',
		'option_d' => 'Use reasonable administrative, technical, and physical safeguards appropriate to the record format and setting, with access limited to authorized persons.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Broad access increases unnecessary disclosure risk.\n\nB. Incorrect. Weak shared credentials undermine security and accountability.\n\nC. Incorrect. Personal unprotected accounts can create avoidable privacy and security risks.\n\nD. Correct. Counselors are responsible for protecting the confidentiality and integrity of records through safeguards proportionate to risk.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB9-C4 Record Security, Storage, and Record Destruction",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC seeks consultation about a difficult case. What information-sharing approach is MOST ethical?',
		'option_a' => 'Provide the entire record so the consultant has every possible detail.',
		'option_b' => 'Post the case in a public online group without removing identifiers.',
		'option_c' => 'Share only information necessary for the consultation, protect identity when feasible, and ensure the consultation setting supports confidentiality.',
		'option_d' => 'Avoid consultation entirely because any professional discussion violates confidentiality.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. A complete record is often unnecessary for focused consultation.\n\nB. Incorrect. Public identifiable disclosure is inconsistent with confidentiality safeguards.\n\nC. Correct. Consultation is ethically permissible when disclosures are limited and confidentiality is protected.\n\nD. Incorrect. Appropriate consultation is an accepted professional activity.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C4 Consultation and Seeking Professional Assistance",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC believes a client meets clinical criteria associated with a 5150 hold. Which statement is MOST accurate?',
		'option_a' => 'Meeting clinical criteria does not automatically mean every LPCC has statutory authority to initiate a hold; the clinician must know the applicable designation and local authority rules and arrange emergency intervention accordingly.',
		'option_b' => 'Every California LPCC automatically has statewide authority to write a 5150 hold.',
		'option_c' => 'Only law enforcement may ever initiate a 5150 hold.',
		'option_d' => 'A client\'s refusal of voluntary hospitalization automatically authorizes any therapist to write the hold.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. California involuntary-detention authority is granted to specified persons and can depend on county designation; clinical assessment and legal authority are related but distinct questions.\n\nB. Incorrect. LPCC licensure alone does not necessarily confer statewide detention authority.\n\nC. Incorrect. The statute includes categories beyond law enforcement.\n\nD. Incorrect. Refusal of voluntary care does not create authority in a person who lacks it.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A parent demands the complete record of a 15-year-old who lawfully self-consented to counseling and whose parent did not participate. What should the LPCC do FIRST?',
		'option_a' => 'Determine the controlling minor-consent and patient-record access rules for the specific records before releasing anything.',
		'option_b' => 'Release the record because parents always control a minor\'s mental health record.',
		'option_c' => 'Refuse permanently because parents can never receive records from self-consented care.',
		'option_d' => 'Destroy sensitive notes so there is no disclosure decision to make.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Minor cases require separate analysis of consent authority, confidentiality, privilege, and record access.\n\nB. Incorrect. Parental status does not automatically resolve all access rights.\n\nC. Incorrect. An absolute prohibition is also too broad; the governing facts and statutes control.\n\nD. Incorrect. Destroying records to avoid an access analysis violates recordkeeping integrity.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB1-C6 Minor Consent Exceptions; WB8-C4 Client Access to Records",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is considering a standardized assessment for a client whose language and cultural background differ from the population on which the tool was validated. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Use the tool exactly as usual because standardized instruments are culture-free.',
		'option_b' => 'Translate items informally during administration without considering scoring validity.',
		'option_c' => 'Refuse all assessment because cultural differences make assessment unethical.',
		'option_d' => 'Evaluate the tool\'s validity and limitations for this client, use culturally and linguistically appropriate methods, and interpret results cautiously within competence.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Standardization does not eliminate cultural limitations.\n\nB. Incorrect. Informal modification can change validity and scoring meaning.\n\nC. Incorrect. Assessment can be ethical when appropriately selected and interpreted.\n\nD. Correct. Assessment ethics require competence, appropriate instrument selection, and attention to cultural and linguistic factors affecting validity.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: License-Specific Module U3; WB7-C1 Cultural Humility",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An LPCC is interested in a new intervention with limited research support. What is the MOST ethical approach?',
		'option_a' => 'Market the intervention as proven because it is popular online.',
		'option_b' => 'Use sound professional judgment, review available evidence and risks, obtain appropriate competence and informed consent, monitor outcomes, and avoid overstating effectiveness.',
		'option_c' => 'Use it without telling clients that evidence is limited.',
		'option_d' => 'Reject every emerging intervention until decades of research exist.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Popularity is not evidence of established effectiveness.\n\nB. Correct. Ethical innovation requires transparency, competence, evidence awareness, and outcome monitoring.\n\nC. Incorrect. Clients need material information about limitations and uncertainty.\n\nD. Incorrect. Ethics does not prohibit responsible innovation.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB3-C3 Maintaining Professional Competence",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'An solo-practice LPCC becomes suddenly incapacitated and cannot contact clients. Which preexisting system would BEST protect clients?',
		'option_a' => 'A plan directing family members to read all records and decide which clients need help.',
		'option_b' => 'No plan, because incapacity cannot be predicted.',
		'option_c' => 'A professional will or continuity plan identifying a qualified records custodian or practice executor, secure access procedures, client notification steps, emergency coverage, and lawful record disposition.',
		'option_d' => 'Automatic public posting of the client list so other therapists can offer services.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Family members are not automatically authorized or qualified to manage confidential clinical records.\n\nB. Incorrect. Foreseeable practice interruption can be planned for even when timing is unknown.\n\nC. Correct. Advance continuity planning protects confidentiality, records, and client access when the clinician cannot act personally.\n\nD. Incorrect. Publishing a client list would create serious confidentiality harm.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB9-C4 Record Security, Storage, and Record Destruction",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'After a child makes a disclosure that creates reasonable suspicion of abuse, the LPCC wants to interview several family members to decide whether the story is true before reporting. What is the BEST response?',
		'option_a' => 'Complete a full family investigation before deciding whether the report threshold is met.',
		'option_b' => 'Ask the alleged perpetrator to consent to the report first.',
		'option_c' => 'Wait until another professional independently confirms the disclosure.',
		'option_d' => 'Make the mandated report based on reasonable suspicion and avoid delaying the report to conduct an independent investigation.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Investigative delay can interfere with the statutory timeline and exceed the reporter's role.\n\nB. Incorrect. The alleged perpetrator's consent is irrelevant to the statutory duty.\n\nC. Incorrect. Independent confirmation is not required.\n\nD. Correct. The mandated reporter's role is to report reasonable suspicion, not to investigate or substantiate the allegation before reporting.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C2 Avoiding and Preventing Client Harm",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'During telehealth intake, what should an LPCC establish in case the video connection fails during a high-risk discussion?',
		'option_a' => 'A backup communication and emergency plan that identifies how to reconnect, how to reach the client, and what emergency resources apply at the client\'s location.',
		'option_b' => 'Assume the platform will not fail because it is HIPAA-compliant.',
		'option_c' => 'Tell the client to call back whenever convenient, regardless of risk.',
		'option_d' => 'Use the counselor\'s personal social-media account as the automatic backup channel.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Technology failure is foreseeable and should be incorporated into telehealth safety and continuity planning.\n\nB. Incorrect. Compliance claims do not guarantee uninterrupted service.\n\nC. Incorrect. Risk can require more immediate and structured follow-up.\n\nD. Incorrect. Backup communication should follow professional privacy and boundary standards.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C6 Technology Risk Management and Compliance",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'Before initiating telehealth with a California client, which action set is MOST complete?',
		'option_a' => 'Confirm that the platform works and begin treatment; ordinary informed consent is enough.',
		'option_b' => 'Require the client to waive all technology and emergency risks.',
		'option_c' => 'Obtain informed consent for telehealth, explain potential risks and limitations, provide the clinician\'s license or registration information, and document reasonable efforts to identify emergency resources near the client.',
		'option_d' => 'Provide only the clinician\'s business phone number because license information is unnecessary.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation' => "A. Incorrect. Technology function alone does not satisfy the telehealth-specific duties.\n\nB. Incorrect. Clients cannot waive away legal and professional obligations.\n\nC. Correct. The regulation identifies telehealth-specific informed consent, risk/limitation disclosure, professional identification, and emergency-resource preparation.\n\nD. Incorrect. The regulation specifically requires license or registration information.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB2-C2 Telehealth Informed Consent",
	),
	array(
		'question_text' => 'A high-risk client has missed multiple sessions and stopped responding. The clinic policy allows discharge after three no-shows. What is the BEST ethical response?',
		'option_a' => 'Apply policy with clinical judgment: attempt reasonable outreach, reassess known risk and continuity needs, provide crisis and referral information as appropriate, document efforts, and avoid mechanical abandonment.',
		'option_b' => 'Close the case immediately because policy eliminates all clinical judgment.',
		'option_c' => 'Keep the case open forever with no outreach because termination is always harmful.',
		'option_d' => 'Send a punitive message stating the client is irresponsible and cannot return.',
		'correct_option' => 'a',
		'explanation' => "A. Correct. Policies guide practice but do not remove the counselor's duty to consider risk, continuity, and avoidable harm.\n\nB. Incorrect. Mechanical discharge can be unsafe in a known high-risk case.\n\nC. Incorrect. Indefinite passive continuation does not solve engagement or risk management.\n\nD. Incorrect. Punitive communication can damage care and professionalism.\n\nCTA Exam Strategy: Primary remediation route: WB5-C3 Beneficence and Promoting Client Welfare",
	),
);
